PI keys us-east-2 PowerShell OpenAI compatible API key docs 2026", "Searching AWS Bedrock Mantle model IDs Kimi K2.5 us-east-2 DeepSeek V3.2 GLM 5 docs"], "request_id": "9e24e603-9bd6-487b-b0e7-091328f4a6fc", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_model_queries": {"queries": ["AWS Bedrock Mantle API keys us-east-2 PowerShell OpenAI compatible API key docs 2026", "AWS Bedrock Mantle model IDs Kimi K2.5 us-east-2 DeepSeek V3.2 GLM 5 docs"], "type": "search_model_queries"}, "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["globe"], "turn_exchange_id": "dd3cbe0a-a446-480c-81ad-104cac821eb4", "working_turn_id": "dd3cbe0a-a446-480c-81ad-104cac821eb4"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yes — **API Keys is the right next place to look**, but don’t create or rotate anything yet.\n\nAnd I want to document one correction immediately because this may be the exact source of the mess:\n\n> **AWS’s current generic API-key support page does not list `us-east-2` (Ohio) among the regions supporting Bedrock API keys**, even though Kimi K2.5 and DeepSeek V3.2 themselves *are* available for in-region inference in `us-east-2`. citeturn179152search10turn179152search1turn179152search3\n\nThat means we may have been conflating **model region support** with **API-key support**. Those are separate dimensions.\n\n## What the screenshot proves\n\nYou are now in:\n\n```text\nAWS account: 280768229421\nConsole: Amazon Bedrock — bedrock-mantle endpoint\nRegion: US East (Ohio)\nRegion ID: us-east-2\nProject scope: default\n```\n\nThe console itself is also explicitly presenting:\n\n- **Models**\n- **Workbench**\n- **API keys**\n- **Live API docs**\n- both the **bedrock-mantle** and **bedrock-runtime** concepts\n\nSo we are absolutely looking at the right AWS service surface.\n\nAWS currently documents Kimi K2.5 as supporting both `bedrock-runtime` and `bedrock-mantle`, with model ID:\n\n```text\nmoonshotai.kimi-k2.5\n```\n\nand it supports in-region inference in both `us-east-1` and `us-east-2`. citeturn179152search1turn179152search3\n\n---\n\n# Don: click **API keys**\n\nLeft side, under **ACCOUNT SCOPE → API keys**.\n\n### Do not create one yet.\n\nI want to see:\n\n- what keys already exist;\n- short-term vs long-term;\n- their state;\n- creation/expiration;\n- whether AWS associates them with a particular region or scope;\n- whether the console gives you any warning in Ohio.\n\n**Do not show me an actual key value.** Key names, IDs, dates, status, and metadata are fine.\n\nSend me that screenshot.\n\n---\n\n# Meanwhile: here is our documented test matrix\n\nThis is what either you or the terminal agent should execute. We will change **one variable at a time**.\n\n| Test | Region | Endpoint | Model | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | `us-east-2` | Mantle | Kimi K2.5 | Current suspected path |\n| B | `us-east-1` | Mantle | Kimi K2.5 | Isolate API-key/region issue |\n| C | `us-east-2` | Runtime | Kimi K2.5 | Isolate Mantle from model |\n| D | `us-east-1` | Runtime | Kimi K2.5 | Known broadly supported control |\n| E | working region | Mantle | DeepSeek V3.2 | Cross-model verification |\n\n**Nothing else changes during each test.**\n\n---\n\n# PowerShell kit — safe diagnostics first\n\nYou can run these yourself.\n\n### 1. AWS CLI identity\n\n```powershell\naws sts get-caller-identity\n```\n\nExpected shape:\n\n```json\n{\n    \"UserId\": \"...\",\n    \"Account\": \"280768229421\",\n    \"Arn\": \"arn:aws:iam::280768229421:user/...\"\n}\n```\n\nNo secret is exposed by this command.\n\n### 2. See what region your CLI thinks it is using\n\n```powershell\naws configure get region\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\n$env:AWS_REGION\n$env:AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\n```\n\nIf those disagree, **document it**. Do not fix it yet.\n\n### 3. Inspect relevant environment variables without printing secrets\n\nUse this instead of echoing the key:\n\n```powershell\nif ($env:OPENAI_API_KEY) {\n    \"OPENAI_API_KEY = PRESENT, length $($env:OPENAI_API_KEY.Length)\"\n} else {\n    \"OPENAI_API_KEY = ABSENT\"\n}\n\nif ($env:AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK) {\n    \"AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK = PRESENT, length $($env:AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK.Length)\"\n} else {\n    \"AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK = ABSENT\"\n}\n\n\"OPENAI_BASE_URL = $env:OPENAI_BASE_URL\"\n\"AWS_REGION = $env:AWS_REGION\"\n\"AWS_DEFAULT_REGION = $env:AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\"\n```\n\nThis could be extremely revealing.\n\nIf, for example, we find:\n\n```text\nOPENAI_BASE_URL = ...us-east-1...\nAWS_REGION = us-east-2\n```\n\nor vice versa, we have a configuration collision.\n\n---\n\n# One thing I do **not** want yet\n\nDo **not** globally set this:\n\n```powershell\n$env:OPENAI_API_KEY = \"...\"\n```\n\nuntil we know which credential we're testing.\n\nAnd definitely don't paste the key into chat.\n\nOnce we establish the active key, we'll put it into the current PowerShell process only, run one microscopic request, and close the shell afterward if desired.\n\n---\n\n# Critical endpoint distinction\n\nAWS currently documents these as separate OpenAI-compatible surfaces:\n\n### Bedrock runtime\n\n```text\nhttps://bedrock-runtime.<region>.amazonaws.com/openai/v1\n```\n\n### Bedrock Mantle\n\n```text\nhttps://bedrock-mantle.<region>.api.aws/v1\n```\n\nAWS explicitly says that when using the OpenAI SDK, the Bedrock API key goes in `OPENAI_API_KEY`, and the corresponding Bedrock URL goes in `OPENAI_BASE_URL`. citeturn179152search5\n\nFor Kimi specifically, AWS's model card gives the model ID as:\n\n```text\nmoonshotai.kimi-k2.5\n```\n\nnot `kimi-k2.5`, not a deployment name, and not an ARN. citeturn179152search1\n\nThat's another place I want us to be ruthless about exact IDs.\n\n---\n\n## Working hypothesis\n\nI now think there are **three especially plausible mix-ups**:\n\n1. **Ohio model availability ≠ Ohio API-key availability.**\n2. We may have mixed **Mantle URLs and runtime URLs** between tests.\n3. We may have used a legitimate model in a legitimate region with a credential mechanism that isn't supported in that exact combination.\n\nThat would perfectly explain the frustrating pattern of:\n\n> “Everything looks configured. Why the hell won't it connect?”\n\nAnd the good news is that this is **testable without redesigning anything**.\n\n**Next move: click `API keys` and send me that screen.** Then I'll tell you exactly what to inspect before we touch a credential."]}, "create_time": 1790215496.732035, "end_turn": true, "id": "8364d9ae-7fab-4525-a26a-495c60bacace", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "p10g1r", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "8l6sco", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "10": {"edited": false, "id": "rigvs1", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "11": {"edited": false, "id": "wtbo3j", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "2": {"edited": false, "id": "bygu9b", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "3": {"edited": false, "id": "epc6z3", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "4": {"edited": false, "id": "o66dde", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "5": {"edited": false, "id": "zuikbx", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "6": {"edited": false, "id": "3a4ytt", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "7": {"edited": false, "id": "ihwdaq", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "8": {"edited": false, "id": "z0kp36", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "9": {"edited": false, "id": "bx86vh", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [{"alt": "([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/api-keys-supported.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "en