inished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Good. We stop here and move **one variable at a time**.\n\n### Step 1 result — documented\n\nYour local AWS identity and region are internally consistent:\n\n```text\nAWS account:     280768229421\nIAM user:        PeteW\nAWS CLI region:  us-east-2\nAWS_REGION:      unset\nAWS_DEFAULT_REGION: unset\n\nAWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK: PRESENT\nLength: 116\n\nOPENAI_API_KEY:  ABSENT\nOPENAI_BASE_URL: unset\n```\n\nThat gives us one important finding:\n\n> Your machine currently has a Bedrock API key loaded as `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK`, but it is **not currently configured for the OpenAI-compatible Mantle API**, because that path expects the Bedrock key in `OPENAI_API_KEY` plus the Mantle base URL in `OPENAI_BASE_URL`. AWS documents those separately. citeturn526976search0turn526976search1\n\n`AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK` is valid for Boto3/Bedrock-style authentication use cases, so its presence is not wrong. It just does **not prove the Mantle/OpenAI-compatible path is configured**. citeturn526976search3turn526976search5\n\n## Step 2 — do only this\n\nWe are **not invoking a model yet**.\n\nWe are only going to prove that the existing 116-character credential can authenticate to the **Ohio Mantle model-list endpoint**.\n\nRun these three commands:\n\n```powershell\n$env:OPENAI_API_KEY = $env:AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK\n```\n\n```powershell\n$env:OPENAI_BASE_URL = \"https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1\"\n```\n\nThen verify configuration **without exposing the key**:\n\n```powershell\nWrite-Host \"OPENAI_API_KEY=PRESENT length=$($env:OPENAI_API_KEY.Length)\"\nWrite-Host \"OPENAI_BASE_URL=$env:OPENAI_BASE_URL\"\n```\n\nYou should see:\n\n```text\nOPENAI_API_KEY=PRESENT length=116\nOPENAI_BASE_URL=https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1\n```\n\n### Stop 