{"queries": ["Amazon Bedrock Nova 2 Lite Converse operation not allowed us-east-2 amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0 ValidationException Operation not allowed", "Amazon Bedrock Nova 2 Lite invoke model API Converse support"], "type": "search_model_queries"}, "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["globe"], "turn_exchange_id": "878de560-13b6-4638-b8b8-3509104b2eb4", "working_turn_id": "878de560-13b6-4638-b8b8-3509104b2eb4"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Aha — **this one is actually useful**.\n\n`ValidationException: Operation not allowed` is **not** a JSON problem anymore. The request reached Bedrock and Bedrock rejected the *operation/model route*.\n\nFor Nova 2 Lite, AWS’s current docs show why this is happening: in US regions, Nova 2 Lite is meant to be invoked through the **US CRIS inference profile**, using:\n\n```text\nus.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n```\n\nrather than the raw foundation-model ID:\n\n```text\namazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n```\n\nAWS explicitly says that for Nova 2 models accessed from a US region, use the `us.` CRIS-prefixed model ID. citeturn758088search7turn758088search2\n\nSo your inventory command was correct: it showed the underlying model exists and is `ACTIVE`.\n\nBut for actual inference, **the callable ID is different**.\n\n## Do one clean Nova 2 test\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0 `\n  --messages file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-messages.json\" `\n  --inference-config file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-inference.json\"\n```\n\nBut since we haven't created those two files yet, let's avoid another quoting circus.\n\nRun these first:\n\n```powershell\n@'\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"user\",\n    \"content\": [\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Return exactly: NOVA_OK\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n'@ | Set-Content \"$env:TEMP\\nova-messages.json\" -Encoding ascii\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```powershell\n@'\n{\n  \"maxTokens\": 20,\n  \"temperature\": 0\n}\n'@ | Set-Content \"$env:TEMP\\nova-inference.json\" -Encoding ascii\n```\n\nThen the actual invocation:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock-runtime converse `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --model-id us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0 `\n  --messages file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-messages.json\" `\n  --inference-config file://\"$env:TEMP\\nova-inference.json\"\n```\n\nAWS currently lists Nova 2 Lite as supporting **Converse**, so this is a valid operation; the likely defect in our prior call was the raw model ID rather than the required US inference-profile ID. citeturn758088search0turn758088search3\n\n### Document the finding like this\n\n```text\nTEST:\nbedrock-runtime Converse\nRegion: us-east-2\nModel ID: amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n\nRESULT:\nValidationException — Operation not allowed\n\nCLASSIFICATION:\nINFERENCE ROUTING / MODEL ID\n\nROOT CAUSE CANDIDATE:\nNova 2 Lite in US regions requires US CRIS inference profile:\nus.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n\nNOT INDICATED:\nCredential failure\nNetwork failure\nBilling failure\nRegion connectivity failure\nJSON parsing failure\n```\n\nAnd this is exactly why the earlier Playground behavior was confusing: **“model exists in region” and “raw model ID is directly invokable” are not the same thing anymore.**\n\nRun that `us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0` test. If it returns `NOVA_OK`, we've cracked the Amazon-native problem too."]}, "create_time": 1790216172.729494, "end_turn": true, "id": "1775cff2-8a0e-4081-bf25-a35a9141bcd9", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "veyxef", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "b7sbtm", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "2": {"edited": false, "id": "nqjux6", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "3": {"edited": false, "i