t"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["**Yes. At this point I would explicitly test region.** The fact that `us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0` still returns **`ValidationException: Operation not allowed`** from Ohio is valuable: AWS documentation says that US inference profile *should* be callable from `us-east-2`, so either cross-region routing is blocked for this principal/account or we have a Nova-specific account restriction. citeturn802587search0turn802587search9\n\nAWS separately publishes a direct Nova 2 Lite example using **N. Virginia (`us-east-1`)** with the raw model ID `amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0`. citeturn802587search5\n\nSo let's do the cleanest A/B test possible.\n\n### Next step — **only test N. Virginia**\n\nFirst, don't change your AWS default region globally. Just issue this control-plane check:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock get-inference-profile `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --inference-profile-identifier us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0\n```\n\n**Stop after that and paste the result.**\n\nThis costs nothing and tells us whether your `PeteW` principal can even resolve the US Nova inference profile from Ohio.\n\nThere are only two meaningful branches:\n\n- **It returns profile details:** Ohio can see the CRIS route, and we investigate invocation/IAM next.\n- **It errors:** we've isolated the failure to **cross-region profile access/routing**, which makes the N. Virginia direct-model test the obvious next control.\n\nAnd importantly, we are **not touching the working Kimi/Mantle configuration**. That path is now sacred until this Nova issue is understood. memcite"]}, "create_time": 1790216359.961739, "end_turn": true, "id": "da4189c5-996f-4a4f-acb7-76c6afe7417c", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "5i9kn3", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [{"alt": "([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/en_en/bedrock/latest/userguide