_title": "Searching the web", "reasoning_titles": ["Searching the web"], "request_id": "aca0a039-415f-403e-988c-02f0f493de8a", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_model_queries": {"queries": ["site:docs.aws.amazon.com Bedrock service quotas invoke model token generating quota 0 account activation Operation not allowed", "site:repost.aws Bedrock \"Operation not allowed\" Converse Nova quota 0"], "type": "search_model_queries"}, "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["globe"], "turn_exchange_id": "cd493a61-561f-4de9-a209-b49976ca1a23", "working_turn_id": "cd493a61-561f-4de9-a209-b49976ca1a23"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Perfect. **That closes the IAM branch.**\n\nCurrent permission picture:\n\n- `PeteW` direct managed policy: `AWSSupportAccess`\n- `PeteW` inline policy: `SupportConsoleAccess`\n- `PeteW` group: `GCE`\n- `GCE` attached managed policy: **`AdministratorAccess`**\n- `GCE` inline policies: **none**\n- `PeteW` permissions boundary: **null**\n\nSo there is no obvious IAM-side reason why `bedrock:InvokeModel` should be denied. AWS documents `InvokeModel` as the permission underlying both direct model invocation and Converse-family access. citeturn338046search3turn338046search6\n\nAt this point, I would **stop changing credentials or IAM entirely**.\n\nThe strongest remaining hypothesis is **Bedrock Runtime quota/account provisioning**, because AWS says `bedrock-runtime` inference is governed by separate per-model quotas, while **Mantle traffic is counted separately**. That fits our exact split: Kimi works through Mantle, while Nova Runtime returns `ValidationException: Operation not allowed`. citeturn338046search1\n\nThere are recent AWS re:Post cases with the same symptom pattern: model listing succeeds, IAM is correct, but Nova Converse returns `Operation not allowed`; in at least one case the account showed **RPM/TPM = 0 for Nova**. That isn't proof your account has the same condition, but it is now the right thing to test. citeturn338046search0turn338046search2\n\n### Next command only\n\nLet's inspect the actual Bedrock service quotas available to your account in Ohio:\n\n```powershell\naws service-quotas list-service-quotas `\n  --service-code bedrock `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"Quotas[?contains(QuotaName, 'Nova')].[QuotaName,Value,Adjustable]\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nIf that throws another table-format annoyance, use:\n\n```powershell\naws service-quotas list-service-quotas `\n  --service-code bedrock `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"Quotas[?contains(QuotaName, 'Nova')]\" `\n  --output json\n```\n\nWhat I care about is whether Nova shows **`0` RPM / `0` TPM** or some other restricted value.\n\nIf it does, we’ve probably found the real root cause:\n\n> **Mantle provisioned/usable; classic Bedrock Runtime token-generating quota not provisioned for Nova on this account.**\n\nThat would also explain why the Playground itself gave you grief even though the model catalog and inference profile looked perfectly healthy."]}, "create_time": 1790216624.541333, "end_turn": true, "id": "af0c213c-3c0f-4008-b5d3-6a5dd656998f", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "9oqmxo", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "znp39u", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_references": [{"alt": "([AWS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/APIReference/API_runtime_InvokeModel.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 568, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "items": [{"attribution": "AWS Documentation", "attribution_segments": null, "attributions": null, "hue": null, "pub_date": null, "refs": [{"ref_index": 3, "ref_type": "search", "turn