untime`**, AWS says to use `list-foundation-models` and `list-inference-profiles`.\n- AWS’s endpoint matrix currently shows Amazon Nova 2 Lite / Nova 2 Sonic as **runtime-supported and Mantle-not-supported**. citeturn239718search0turn239718search5\n\nSo your “Amazon model problem” may simply have been:\n\n> **trying to use an Amazon-native Nova model through the Mantle/OpenAI path that successfully serves Kimi.**\n\nThat would explain the Playground confusion.\n\n## Next step — runtime inventory only\n\nDo **not** invoke anything yet.\n\nRun this in PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"modelSummaries[?contains(providerName, 'Amazon')].[modelId,modelName,inferenceTypesSupported]\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nIf that returns nothing, run the broader version:\n\n```powershell\naws bedrock list-foundation-models `\n  --region us-east-2 `\n  --query \"modelSummaries[].[providerName,modelId,modelName,inferenceTypesSupported]\" `\n  --output table\n```\n\nPaste the Amazon rows back.\n\n### What we've documented now\n\n**Mantle control lane:**\n`us-east-2 → bedrock-mantle → Kimi K2.5 → WORKING`\n\n**Amazon-native lane:**\n`us-east-2 → Mantle /models → no Amazon/Nova entries`\n\n**Next question:**\nDoes `bedrock-runtime` expose Nova directly or through an inference profile?\n\nAWS currently recommends `bedrock-runtime` for new applications where the required model is supported, so this is the correct fork to inspect. citeturn239718search2turn239718search4\n\nWe're not changing the working Kimi setup. We're just proving the **second lane** separately."]}, "create_time": 1790215879.33949, "end_turn": true, "id": "255d9e4e-e2e0-4b21-b3cc-b630b60a09e8", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "x0gg1t", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}, "1": {"edited": false, "id": "xtvf3s", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, "content_refere