     ↓\nTerminal / OpenCode / local agent runner\n        ↓\nLocal filesystem access\n        ↓\nBedrock Mantle provider\n        ↓\nhttps://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1\n        ↓\nKimi / GLM / DeepSeek / Qwen / other verified models\n```\n\nThe model should receive file context through the **terminal tool/orchestrator**, not by trying to make Bedrock itself browse your PC.\n\nThat distinction matters:\n\n**Bedrock supplies inference.  \nThe terminal/orchestrator supplies file access, tools, shell, repo context, diffs, and execution.**\n\nThat is exactly the bridge we want.\n\nLuce’s minimum corrective action matches this: add one OpenAI-compatible GCE consumer pointed at Mantle using the existing bearer credential, starting with a verified low-cost model. fileciteturn0file0L100-L105\n\n## What I would make the terminal team build\n\nOne **custom Bedrock-Mantle provider** in the terminal stack, with model aliases rather than separate hand-built integrations for every model.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nprovider: aws-bedrock-mantle\n\nbase_url:\nhttps://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1\n\nauth:\nAWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK\n\nmodels:\n  kimi25  -> moonshotai.kimi-k2.5\n  kthink  -> moonshotai.kimi-k2-thinking\n  glm5    -> zai.glm-5\n  glm47   -> zai.glm-4.7\n  glmflash-> zai.glm-4.7-flash\n  ds32    -> deepseek.v3.2\n  qcoder  -> qwen.qwen3-coder-480b-a35b-instruct\n```\n\nThen the **existing terminal agent** keeps responsibility for:\n\n- reading local files;\n- searching repos;\n- applying patches;\n- running PowerShell/Bash;\n- preserving GCE seat identity;\n- routing tasks;\n- passing context to whichever Bedrock model is selected.\n\nNo new orchestration philosophy is needed.\n\n## Best initial GCE lanes\n\nLuce’s verified capability map is already strong:\n\n- **Heavy reasoning:** Kimi K2.5 / K2 Thinking\n- **Coding/build:** Qwen3 Coder 480B / Coder Next\n- **Architecture/review:** GLM 5\n- **Low-cost utility:** GLM 4.7 Flash\n- **Verification:** DeepSeek V3.2\n- **Experimental:** MiniMax M2.5 / GLM 4.6 / Qwen 235B fileciteturn0file0L42-L49\n\nThat is nearly a complete terminal bench already.\n\n## One correction to Luce’s report\n\nLuce could not retrieve the AWS credits programmatically and therefore treated the historical balance as unverified. fileciteturn0file0L12-L15\n\nWe subsequently verified manually in the AWS console that the live balance is **$159.26**.\n\nSo the current continuity picture is actually stronger than Luce’s report:\n\n**Inference path:** verified  \n**Model availability:** verified  \n**Local credentials:** verified  \n**IAM:** verified  \n**Credits:** manually verified  \n**Remaining blocker:** local provider wiring\n\n## Separate later action item\n\nI’m keeping this isolated from the bridge work:\n\n**Later: request/enable Nova / classic Bedrock Runtime quotas.**\n\nYour account-level Nova Runtime quotas are zero, so we do not let that sidetrack the Mantle integration.\n\n## What I would hand terminal/Nico\n\nThe next implementation mission should be very narrow:\n\n> **Wire one reversible Bedrock Mantle provider into the existing terminal/OpenCode execution path, prove local-file access with Kimi K2.5, then expose model aliases for the verified Mantle estate. Do not touch seat doctrine, IAM, AWS credentials, or Nova Runtime.**\n\nAcceptance test should be something real, not `BEDROCK_OK`:\n\n```text\nUse Bedrock Kimi K2.5 from terminal.\nRead a known local GCE file.\nSummarize one exact section.\nReport the file path used.\nMake no changes.\n```\n\nIf that passes, then we have what you actually wanted:\n\n> **AWS becomes the emergency/continuity compute layer behind the GCE Orchestrator, with full terminal file access, bridging gaps in Kimi, GLM, and OpenAI subscription availability.**\n\nAnd Luce independently reached the same continuity conclusion: one config-only bridge converts Bedrock from latent capability into a usable GCE fallback. fileciteturn0file0L114-L127\n\n**Status: PROCEED.** The AWS investigation is basically done; the next problem is local integration. memcite"]}, "create_time": 1790217008.189985, "end_turn": true, "id": "b61c9803-7323-42fa-b288-d4c6b6a50f9c", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 332, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "9233084a-7ac9-4763-85e5-e36b1a2ccdef", "cited_message_idx": 214, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_946b6a34ceec8191af28738f6dbbb636", "line_range": [6, 15], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 0, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000067088230a6b8119a3fcae9cf", "name": "Pasted text.txt", "source": "my_files", "text": "A. VERDICT\nBEDROCK OPERATIONAL:       PARTIAL — Mantle path FULLY OPERATIONAL; Classic runtime BLOCKED\nPRIMARY WORKING ENDPOINT:  https://bedrock-mantle.us-east-2.api.aws/v1 (Chat Completions API)\n...", "type": "file"}, "start_ix": 304}, {"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 741, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "9233084a-7ac9-4763-85e5-e36b1a2ccdef", "cited_message_idx": 214, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_946b6a34ceec8191af28738f6dbbb636", "line_range": [69, 87], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retrieval_turn": 0, "run_id": null, "search_result_idx": null, "set_id": null, "source_icon_url": null, "source_label": null, "source_url": null, "spl_version": null, "title": null}, "id": "file_0000000067088230a6b8119a3fcae9cf", 